# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hume-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: S3 is read 2 ways: hume-treatise-2.logic.r2 has "convinced → loose ∧ receive_extraordinary"; hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 and hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 have "convinced → loose" and "loose → receive_extraordinary"; they differ when only "men are thrown loose from all common systems" is true, which the first allows
hume-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: S3: answered yes, which keeps hume-treatise-2.logic.r2, to: Suppose "men are thrown loose from all common systems" is true and "men are once fully persuaded of the two principles: that nothing in any object, considered in itself, affords a reason for drawing a conclusion beyond it, and that even after observing frequent or constant conjunction we have no reason to infer beyond what we have experienced" and "men will make no difficulty of receiving any system, however extraordinary it may appear" are false. Is S3, "Let men be once fully perswaded of these two principles, THAT THERE, IS NOTHING IN ANY OBJECT, CONSIDERED IN ITSELF, WHICH CAN AFFORD US A REASON FOR DRAWING A CONCLUSION BEYOND it; and, THAT EVEN AFTER THE OBSERVATION OF THE FREQUENT OR CONSTANT CONJUNCTION OF OBJECTS, WE HAVE NO REASON TO DRAW ANY INFERENCE CONCERNING ANY OBJECT BEYOND THOSE OF WHICH WE HAVE HAD EXPERIENCE; I say, let men be once fully convinced of these two principles, and this will throw them so loose from all common systems, that they will make no difficulty of receiving any, which may appear the most extraordinary.", true then?
hume-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: S4 is read 2 ways: hume-treatise-2.logic.r2 and hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 have "convincing_causal"; hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 has "convincing_causal" and "evidence_probable"; they differ when only "the two principles have been found sufficiently convincing with regard to our most certain reasonings from causation" is true, which the first allows
hume-treatise-2.md:1: argue/answered: S4: answered no, which keeps hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "the two principles have been found sufficiently convincing with regard to our most certain reasonings from causation" is true and "with regard to conjectural or probable reasonings the two principles acquire a new degree of evidence" is false. Is S4, "These principles we have found to be sufficiently convincing, even with regard to our most certain reasonings from causation: But I shall venture to affirm, that with regard to these conjectural or probable reasonings they still acquire a new degree of evidence.", true then?
hume-treatise-2.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hume-treatise-2.logic.r2 and hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 have "evidence_probable"; hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 has "convinced → receive_extraordinary"; they differ when only "with regard to conjectural or probable reasonings the two principles acquire a new degree of evidence" and "men are once fully persuaded of the two principles: that nothing in any object, considered in itself, affords a reason for drawing a conclusion beyond it, and that even after observing frequent or constant conjunction we have no reason to infer beyond what we have experienced" are true, which the first allows
hume-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: the main conclusion is read at 2 sentences: hume-treatise-2.logic.r2 and hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.2 put it at S4, "These principles we have found to be sufficiently convincing, even with regard to our most certain reasonings from causation: But I shall venture to affirm, that with regard to these conjectural or probable reasonings they still acquire a new degree of evidence."; hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.3 at S3, "Let men be once fully perswaded of these two principles, THAT THERE, IS NOTHING IN ANY OBJECT, CONSIDERED IN ITSELF, WHICH CAN AFFORD US A REASON FOR DRAWING A CONCLUSION BEYOND it; and, THAT EVEN AFTER THE OBSERVATION OF THE FREQUENT OR CONSTANT CONJUNCTION OF OBJECTS, WE HAVE NO REASON TO DRAW ANY INFERENCE CONCERNING ANY OBJECT BEYOND THOSE OF WHICH WE HAVE HAD EXPERIENCE; I say, let men be once fully convinced of these two principles, and this will throw them so loose from all common systems, that they will make no difficulty of receiving any, which may appear the most extraordinary."
hume-treatise-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: M: answered no, which keeps hume-treatise-2.logic.r2.3, to: Is the text's main conclusion S4, "These principles we have found to be sufficiently convincing, even with regard to our most certain reasonings from causation: But I shall venture to affirm, that with regard to these conjectural or probable reasonings they still acquire a new degree of evidence.", rather than S3, "Let men be once fully perswaded of these two principles, THAT THERE, IS NOTHING IN ANY OBJECT, CONSIDERED IN ITSELF, WHICH CAN AFFORD US A REASON FOR DRAWING A CONCLUSION BEYOND it; and, THAT EVEN AFTER THE OBSERVATION OF THE FREQUENT OR CONSTANT CONJUNCTION OF OBJECTS, WE HAVE NO REASON TO DRAW ANY INFERENCE CONCERNING ANY OBJECT BEYOND THOSE OF WHICH WE HAVE HAD EXPERIENCE; I say, let men be once fully convinced of these two principles, and this will throw them so loose from all common systems, that they will make no difficulty of receiving any, which may appear the most extraordinary."?
